\begin{lemma}[A compact round backward limit]
\label{lem:ancient-compact-round-limit}
If a prescribed backward rescaling sequence of a three-dimensional
ancient $\kappa$-solution has a compact round limit, then every
slice of the original solution has constant positive sectional
curvature, including time zero.
\lean{PoincareMT.AncientKappaRoundness.isRoundAncientKappaSolution_of_compact_round_limit}
\uses{prop:ancient-sequence}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Compactness makes late convergence embeddings global. Curvature convergence
gives arbitrarily sharp round pinching on increasingly early slices of the
original flow. The tensor maximum principle preserves each pinching cone
forward. Intersecting all cones forces an Einstein metric on every slice,
hence constant positive sectional curvature in dimension three.
\end{proof}

